  "title": "OCD: The Cortico-Striato-Thalamic Trap",
  "emoji": "🩺",
  "color": "#5B21B6",
  "description": "A tiny lesson with a big payoff: OCD: The Cortico-Striato-Thalamic Trap.",
  "is_free": false,
  "published": true,
  "story": {
    "hook": {
      "text": "The brain has a three-station loop that filters urges: cortex, striatum, thalamus. In OCD, that loop runs wild.",
      "visual": "🔺"
    },
    "buildup": {
      "text": "Normally the orbitofrontal cortex flags a concern, the striatum evaluates it, and the thalamus decides whether to act or ignore.",
      "visual": "🧠"
    },
    "discovery": {
      "text": "In OCD the thalamus relays the alarm signal back to the cortex, creating a self-reinforcing cycle of anxiety and compulsion.",
      "visual": "🔄"
    },
    "twist": {
      "text": "Serotonin modulates this circuit. SSRIs can dampen the overactive loop, but they take weeks to reach full effect.",
      "visual": "💊"
    },
    "climax": {
      "text": "When medication and therapy both fail, surgeons can interrupt the circuit with precise lesions or deep brain stimulation.",
      "visual": "⚡"
    },
    "punchline": {
      "text": "OCD is not a personality quirk. It is a three-node circuit that forgot how to say stop.",
      "visual": "🛑"
    }
  },
  "quiz": {
    "question": "What three brain structures form the loop disrupted in OCD?",
    "options": [
      "The cortex, striatum, and thalamus",
      "The hippocampus, amygdala, and cerebellum",
      "The brainstem, spinal cord, and cortex"
    ],
    "correct": 0
  },
